{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0119DTXzw3uJ8shKttiXmWjG","type":"tool_result","content":"./README.md\n./launchctl-print.txt\n./bin/nightly-review.zsh\n./bin/notify.sh\n./notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n./notes/two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent.md\n./notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n./Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n./Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\n./Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n\n=== ./README.md\n# nightly-review (snapshot of my home dir, the relevant bits)\n\nA launchd user agent runs `bin/nightly-review.zsh` every night at 02:30. The\nscript asks a headless coding agent to review yesterday's commits in\n~/projects/shop and sends me the summary (or a failure note) over Telegram\nvia `bin/notify.sh`.\n\nLayout, relative to my home:\n\n- `Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist` — loaded with\n  `launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ...` on 2026-10-12\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh` — the job\n- `bin/notify.sh` — Telegram notifier (works when run by hand)\n- `Library/Logs/` — where the plist points stdout/stderr\n- `launchctl-print.txt` — output of `launchctl print gui/501/com.vk.nightly-review`, captured 2026-10-18 09:10\n\nI have received nothing on Telegram since it was loaded: no summaries, no\nfailure notes.\n=== ./launchctl-print.txt\ngui/501/com.vk.nightly-review = {\n\tactive count = 0\n\tpath = /Users/vk/Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n\ttype = LaunchAgent\n\tstate = waiting\n\n\tprogram = /bin/zsh\n\targuments = {\n\t\t/bin/zsh\n\t\t/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh\n\t}\n\n\tstdout path = /Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n\tstderr path = /Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\n\tdefault environment = {\n\t\tPATH => /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\n\t}\n\n\tenvironment = {\n\t\tPATH => /opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\n\t\tXPC_SERVICE_NAME => com.vk.nightly-review\n\t}\n\n\tdomain = gui/501 [100005]\n\tminimum runtime = 10\n\texit timeout = 5\n\truns = 6\n\tlast exit code = 1\n\n\tevent triggers = {\n\t\tcom.vk.nightly-review.StartCalendarInterval => {\n\t\t\tkeep alive = 0\n\t\t\tservice = com.vk.nightly-review\n\t\t\tname = com.vk.nightly-review.StartCalendarInterval\n\t\t\tdescriptor = {\n\t\t\t\t\"Hour\" => 2\n\t\t\t\t\"Minute\" => 30\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tspawn type = daemon (3)\n\tproperties = {\n\t\tpartial import = 0\n\t\tlaunchd bundle = 0\n\t\ttrusted = 0\n\t\ton demand = 1\n\t}\n}\n=== ./bin/nightly-review.zsh\n#!/bin/zsh\n# Nightly review of yesterday's commits, summary to Telegram.\nset -u\n\nREPO=\"$HOME/projects/shop\"\nOUT=\"$HOME/Library/Logs/nightly-review-$(date +%F).md\"\nNOTIFY=\"$HOME/bin/notify.sh\"\n\nSINCE=$(date -v-1d +%F)\nPROMPT=\"Review the commits in this repository since $SINCE.\nFor each commit: one line on what it changes and whether anything looks risky\n(migrations, auth, payments, deleted tests).\nEnd with a three-line summary I can read on my phone.\nDo not modify any file.\"\n\ncd \"$REPO\" || { \"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review: repo missing\"; exit 1; }\n\nclaude -p \"$PROMPT\" --output-format text > \"$OUT\"\nstatus=$?\n\nif [ $status -ne 0 ]; then\n  \"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review FAILED (exit $status), see $OUT\"\n  exit $status\nfi\n\n\"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review done: $(head -c 300 \"$OUT\")\"\n=== ./bin/notify.sh\n#!/bin/bash\n# usage: notify.sh \"message\"  — sends a Telegram message to me.\nset -eu\n: \"${TG_BOT_TOKEN:=$(security find-generic-password -s tg-bot -w)}\"\nCHAT_ID=123456789\ncurl -sS -X POST \"https://api.telegram.org/bot${TG_BOT_TOKEN}/sendMessage\" \\\n  -d chat_id=\"$CHAT_ID\" --data-urlencode text=\"$1\" > /dev/null\n=== ./notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `<path>` instead — guaranteed\n  everywhere.\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\n\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\nclick-to-copy version inside the app too, but the embed is just markdown, so it\nworks anywhere markdown does. The badges are already on my own repo's README,\ndoing their little double duty.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a web app/API you control that stores a public, per-item stat\n(reproductions, stars, downloads, streak — anything already public), and a way\nto add a public HTTP route.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which stat to surface, where it lives (your DB/\nstore), your brand colors, and your public base URL.\n\nSteps:\n1. Add a **public** `GET /badge/{id}.svg` route that looks up the item's stat.\n   No auth — the badge must be embeddable by anyone; only expose already-public\n   numbers.\n2. Return a two-segment \"shields-style\" SVG: a dark label segment (your app\n   name) + a colored value segment (the stat). Compute each segment's width from\n   its text length so it never clips. Set `Content-Type: image/svg+xml`.\n3. Draw any icon (✓, ★) as an inline `<path>`, never a unicode character — text\n   glyphs depend on the viewer's fonts and show as boxes in some renderers. Keep\n   the SVG fully self-contained (no external fonts or images).\n4. Add a short `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=300`). GitHub's image\n   proxy will cache it regardless; this just keeps the source cheap.\n5. Hand users the embed line — the trailing link is the backlink that promotes\n   your app:\n   `[![yourapp](https://yourapp.com/badge/{id}.svg)](https://yourapp.com/item/{id})`\n6. Optional: show the same embed as click-to-copy in your app's UI so users find\n   it without reading docs.\n\nVerify: paste the badge into a GitHub README. It must render as a crisp badge —\nnot a broken-image icon, and no `□` where the icon should be. Confirm the number\nmatches your app. Then bump the item's stat and confirm the badge updates after\nGitHub's cache window (a README edit busts it immediately).\n\nCaveat to state up front: because of GitHub's camo cache, treat the count as\n\"eventually fresh,\" not real-time — and keep the SVG self-contained, or camo\nwill render a broken image.\n=== ./notes/two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent.md\n---\n\n# Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\n\nYesterday I filed an open failure: my launchd job — a daily 16:50 headless-agent\nreview — produced nothing. No notification, no output, and, I claimed, no evidence\nit ran at all.\n\nI was wrong about the last part, and finding out how was the whole lesson.\n\nOne command answered it: `launchctl print gui/$UID/<job-label>`. It showed\n`runs = 2` and `last exit code = 1`. The job had fired, on schedule, both days.\nThe log file I was sure did not exist had existed the whole time. I had been\ndebugging \"launchd is not firing\" when the truth was \"my script fails and cannot\ntell me.\"\n\nThe log revealed two stacked bugs. First, my headless agent call wasn't receiving\nits prompt in the launchd context, so the review died immediately (\"Input must be\nprovided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\"). Fix: pipe the prompt via\nstdin — `print -r -- \"$PROMPT\" | claude -p ...` — instead of trusting multi-line\nargument parsing inside a scheduled shell.\n\nSecond — the one that made everything invisible — my script assigned `status=$?`.\nIn zsh, `status` is a read-only built-in variable, and assigning to it kills the\nscript on the spot. That assignment sat between the agent call and the\nfailure-notification call. So the job failed, and then the messenger that was\nsupposed to report failures died before speaking. Perfect silence, two layers\ndeep.\n\nThe takeaway I will keep: when automation is silent, first prove whether it ran\n(`launchctl print` shows the runs count and the last exit code), and then check\nwhether your failure reporter is downstream of the failure. Mine was. Everyone's\nis, until they check.\n\nCorrection, 2026-09-30: I originally wrote that the runs count and last exit code\n\"do not lie\". In my case the exit code happened to be honest (1). It is not\nproof in general: a script can exit 0 and have done nothing, for example a\nwatchdog that exits 0 on timeout. The exit code tells you *that* it ran, not\n*that it worked*. Check the real outcome (the file, the message, the row it was\nsupposed to produce) and how long the run took; a headless agent run that ends\nin a few seconds almost always means it failed before doing any work. Two more\ntraps for `claude -p` under launchd, from\n[Mihai Perdum's seven silent launchd failures](https://dev.to/mihai_leanzero/your-unattended-ai-agent-looks-healthy-and-does-nothing-7-silent-failures-on-macos-launchd-2026-4gnm):\ngive it a closed stdin (`< /dev/null`) when the prompt is an argument, or it\nwaits on stdin; and set `USER` in the plist's `EnvironmentVariables`, or the\nCLI can't find its login in the keychain and dies with \"Not logged in\".\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a macOS launchd user agent wrapping any long-running or headless\ncommand.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the job label, the wrapper script path.\n\nSteps: check reality first with launchctl print gui/$UID/<label> (runs, last exit\ncode, state); read the StandardErrorPath log before theorizing; in zsh scripts\nnever assign to `status` — use `rc=$?`; pass prompts/config to headless tools via\nstdin rather than multi-line arguments (or, if you pass the prompt as an\nargument, add `< /dev/null`); set `USER` under `EnvironmentVariables` in the plist\nso a headless CLI can reach its keychain login; log each run's duration; put the\nfailure notification as close to the top of the failure path as possible, and\ntest it by forcing a failure once.\n\nVerify: launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/<label>, then confirm last exit code = 0\nAND the real outcome exists and is fresh (the output file, message or row the job\nproduces) AND the run took a plausible time, not a few seconds; confirm you\nreceived the success notification. Then break the script on purpose and confirm\nyou receive the failure notification too. Silence should be impossible in both\ndirections, and so should a green exit code with nothing done.\n=== ./notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n---\n\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\n\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\ndatabase, no build step.\n\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\n\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\nconfusion.\n\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\n\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\nthe email are the confirmation, not the fetch.\n\nSimple things should stay simple.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a static site you can edit and deploy, and a Google account.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which spreadsheet to write into (or permission\nto create one), the address to notify, and the list of fields to collect.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create a Google Sheet for submissions. Its ID is the long string in the URL\n   between `/d/` and `/edit`.\n2. In that sheet choose Extensions, then Apps Script. Write a `doPost(e)` that\n   parses `e.postData.contents` as JSON; returns success immediately and writes\n   nothing if the honeypot field is non-empty; rejects missing required fields;\n   takes a `LockService.getScriptLock()` before writing so two submissions in\n   the same second cannot interleave; appends a row via\n   `SpreadsheetApp.openById(<id>).getSheets()[0].appendRow([...])` with a\n   timestamp first; releases the lock; then sends a notification with\n   `MailApp.sendEmail`. Truncate every field to a maximum length before writing.\n3. Deploy, New deployment, type \"Web app\", Execute as **Me**, Who has access\n   **Anyone**. Authorise it. Copy the URL ending in `/exec`.\n4. In the page, add the form with the real fields, plus a hidden input that no\n   human can see or tab into (`tabindex=\"-1\"`, `aria-hidden=\"true\"`) as the\n   honeypot, and a status element with `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"`.\n5. Validate in the browser first, setting `aria-invalid` on each field and\n   focusing the first bad one, so a screen reader announces the problem.\n6. Submit with `fetch(url, { method: \"POST\", mode: \"no-cors\", headers: {\n   \"Content-Type\": \"text/plain;charset=utf-8\" }, body: JSON.stringify(data) })`.\n   Do not use `application/json`: it triggers a preflight that Apps Script\n   never answers. Because the response is opaque, treat the resolved promise as\n   success and let the sheet and the email be the source of truth.\n7. Read `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign` from the query string, fall\n   back to `document.referrer`, and send that as a `source` field.\n8. When you later change the script, use Deploy, Manage deployments, the pencil\n   icon, and set Version to \"New version\". A fresh deployment would give you a\n   different `/exec` URL and silently break the live form.\n\nVerify: submit the form on the deployed site. A row appears in the sheet within\na few seconds and the notification email arrives. Then fill the honeypot field\nfrom devtools and submit again: no new row appears, and the endpoint still\nreports success, so a bot sees nothing unusual.\n=== ./Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n=== ./Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\n=== ./Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\" \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n<plist version=\"1.0\">\n<dict>\n  <key>Label</key>\n  <string>com.vk.nightly-review</string>\n  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n  <array>\n    <string>/bin/zsh</string>\n    <string>/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh</string>\n  </array>\n  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>Hour</key>\n    <integer>2</integer>\n    <key>Minute</key>\n    <integer>30</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err</string>\n  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>PATH</key>\n    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>\n  </dict>\n</dict>\n</plist>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a0ad04f-3f18-42c5-a87a-e0825374b1bf","uuid":"661bb670-3fb3-48b3-ba33-f395b7c6a31f","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:14:53.264Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"./README.md\n./launchctl-print.txt\n./bin/nightly-review.zsh\n./bin/notify.sh\n./notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n./notes/two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent.md\n./notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n./Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n./Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\n./Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n\n=== ./README.md\n# nightly-review (snapshot of my home dir, the relevant bits)\n\nA launchd user agent runs `bin/nightly-review.zsh` every night at 02:30. The\nscript asks a headless coding agent to review yesterday's commits in\n~/projects/shop and sends me the summary (or a failure note) over Telegram\nvia `bin/notify.sh`.\n\nLayout, relative to my home:\n\n- `Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist` — loaded with\n  `launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ...` on 2026-10-12\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh` — the job\n- `bin/notify.sh` — Telegram notifier (works when run by hand)\n- `Library/Logs/` — where the plist points stdout/stderr\n- `launchctl-print.txt` — output of `launchctl print gui/501/com.vk.nightly-review`, captured 2026-10-18 09:10\n\nI have received nothing on Telegram since it was loaded: no summaries, no\nfailure notes.\n=== ./launchctl-print.txt\ngui/501/com.vk.nightly-review = {\n\tactive count = 0\n\tpath = /Users/vk/Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n\ttype = LaunchAgent\n\tstate = waiting\n\n\tprogram = /bin/zsh\n\targuments = {\n\t\t/bin/zsh\n\t\t/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh\n\t}\n\n\tstdout path = /Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n\tstderr path = /Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\n\tdefault environment = {\n\t\tPATH => /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\n\t}\n\n\tenvironment = {\n\t\tPATH => /opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\n\t\tXPC_SERVICE_NAME => com.vk.nightly-review\n\t}\n\n\tdomain = gui/501 [100005]\n\tminimum runtime = 10\n\texit timeout = 5\n\truns = 6\n\tlast exit code = 1\n\n\tevent triggers = {\n\t\tcom.vk.nightly-review.StartCalendarInterval => {\n\t\t\tkeep alive = 0\n\t\t\tservice = com.vk.nightly-review\n\t\t\tname = com.vk.nightly-review.StartCalendarInterval\n\t\t\tdescriptor = {\n\t\t\t\t\"Hour\" => 2\n\t\t\t\t\"Minute\" => 30\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tspawn type = daemon (3)\n\tproperties = {\n\t\tpartial import = 0\n\t\tlaunchd bundle = 0\n\t\ttrusted = 0\n\t\ton demand = 1\n\t}\n}\n=== ./bin/nightly-review.zsh\n#!/bin/zsh\n# Nightly review of yesterday's commits, summary to Telegram.\nset -u\n\nREPO=\"$HOME/projects/shop\"\nOUT=\"$HOME/Library/Logs/nightly-review-$(date +%F).md\"\nNOTIFY=\"$HOME/bin/notify.sh\"\n\nSINCE=$(date -v-1d +%F)\nPROMPT=\"Review the commits in this repository since $SINCE.\nFor each commit: one line on what it changes and whether anything looks risky\n(migrations, auth, payments, deleted tests).\nEnd with a three-line summary I can read on my phone.\nDo not modify any file.\"\n\ncd \"$REPO\" || { \"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review: repo missing\"; exit 1; }\n\nclaude -p \"$PROMPT\" --output-format text > \"$OUT\"\nstatus=$?\n\nif [ $status -ne 0 ]; then\n  \"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review FAILED (exit $status), see $OUT\"\n  exit $status\nfi\n\n\"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review done: $(head -c 300 \"$OUT\")\"\n=== ./bin/notify.sh\n#!/bin/bash\n# usage: notify.sh \"message\"  — sends a Telegram message to me.\nset -eu\n: \"${TG_BOT_TOKEN:=$(security find-generic-password -s tg-bot -w)}\"\nCHAT_ID=123456789\ncurl -sS -X POST \"https://api.telegram.org/bot${TG_BOT_TOKEN}/sendMessage\" \\\n  -d chat_id=\"$CHAT_ID\" --data-urlencode text=\"$1\" > /dev/null\n=== ./notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `<path>` instead — guaranteed\n  everywhere.\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\n\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\nclick-to-copy version inside the app too, but the embed is just markdown, so it\nworks anywhere markdown does. The badges are already on my own repo's README,\ndoing their little double duty.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a web app/API you control that stores a public, per-item stat\n(reproductions, stars, downloads, streak — anything already public), and a way\nto add a public HTTP route.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which stat to surface, where it lives (your DB/\nstore), your brand colors, and your public base URL.\n\nSteps:\n1. Add a **public** `GET /badge/{id}.svg` route that looks up the item's stat.\n   No auth — the badge must be embeddable by anyone; only expose already-public\n   numbers.\n2. Return a two-segment \"shields-style\" SVG: a dark label segment (your app\n   name) + a colored value segment (the stat). Compute each segment's width from\n   its text length so it never clips. Set `Content-Type: image/svg+xml`.\n3. Draw any icon (✓, ★) as an inline `<path>`, never a unicode character — text\n   glyphs depend on the viewer's fonts and show as boxes in some renderers. Keep\n   the SVG fully self-contained (no external fonts or images).\n4. Add a short `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=300`). GitHub's image\n   proxy will cache it regardless; this just keeps the source cheap.\n5. Hand users the embed line — the trailing link is the backlink that promotes\n   your app:\n   `[![yourapp](https://yourapp.com/badge/{id}.svg)](https://yourapp.com/item/{id})`\n6. Optional: show the same embed as click-to-copy in your app's UI so users find\n   it without reading docs.\n\nVerify: paste the badge into a GitHub README. It must render as a crisp badge —\nnot a broken-image icon, and no `□` where the icon should be. Confirm the number\nmatches your app. Then bump the item's stat and confirm the badge updates after\nGitHub's cache window (a README edit busts it immediately).\n\nCaveat to state up front: because of GitHub's camo cache, treat the count as\n\"eventually fresh,\" not real-time — and keep the SVG self-contained, or camo\nwill render a broken image.\n=== ./notes/two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent.md\n---\n\n# Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\n\nYesterday I filed an open failure: my launchd job — a daily 16:50 headless-agent\nreview — produced nothing. No notification, no output, and, I claimed, no evidence\nit ran at all.\n\nI was wrong about the last part, and finding out how was the whole lesson.\n\nOne command answered it: `launchctl print gui/$UID/<job-label>`. It showed\n`runs = 2` and `last exit code = 1`. The job had fired, on schedule, both days.\nThe log file I was sure did not exist had existed the whole time. I had been\ndebugging \"launchd is not firing\" when the truth was \"my script fails and cannot\ntell me.\"\n\nThe log revealed two stacked bugs. First, my headless agent call wasn't receiving\nits prompt in the launchd context, so the review died immediately (\"Input must be\nprovided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\"). Fix: pipe the prompt via\nstdin — `print -r -- \"$PROMPT\" | claude -p ...` — instead of trusting multi-line\nargument parsing inside a scheduled shell.\n\nSecond — the one that made everything invisible — my script assigned `status=$?`.\nIn zsh, `status` is a read-only built-in variable, and assigning to it kills the\nscript on the spot. That assignment sat between the agent call and the\nfailure-notification call. So the job failed, and then the messenger that was\nsupposed to report failures died before speaking. Perfect silence, two layers\ndeep.\n\nThe takeaway I will keep: when automation is silent, first prove whether it ran\n(`launchctl print` shows the runs count and the last exit code), and then check\nwhether your failure reporter is downstream of the failure. Mine was. Everyone's\nis, until they check.\n\nCorrection, 2026-09-30: I originally wrote that the runs count and last exit code\n\"do not lie\". In my case the exit code happened to be honest (1). It is not\nproof in general: a script can exit 0 and have done nothing, for example a\nwatchdog that exits 0 on timeout. The exit code tells you *that* it ran, not\n*that it worked*. Check the real outcome (the file, the message, the row it was\nsupposed to produce) and how long the run took; a headless agent run that ends\nin a few seconds almost always means it failed before doing any work. Two more\ntraps for `claude -p` under launchd, from\n[Mihai Perdum's seven silent launchd failures](https://dev.to/mihai_leanzero/your-unattended-ai-agent-looks-healthy-and-does-nothing-7-silent-failures-on-macos-launchd-2026-4gnm):\ngive it a closed stdin (`< /dev/null`) when the prompt is an argument, or it\nwaits on stdin; and set `USER` in the plist's `EnvironmentVariables`, or the\nCLI can't find its login in the keychain and dies with \"Not logged in\".\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a macOS launchd user agent wrapping any long-running or headless\ncommand.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the job label, the wrapper script path.\n\nSteps: check reality first with launchctl print gui/$UID/<label> (runs, last exit\ncode, state); read the StandardErrorPath log before theorizing; in zsh scripts\nnever assign to `status` — use `rc=$?`; pass prompts/config to headless tools via\nstdin rather than multi-line arguments (or, if you pass the prompt as an\nargument, add `< /dev/null`); set `USER` under `EnvironmentVariables` in the plist\nso a headless CLI can reach its keychain login; log each run's duration; put the\nfailure notification as close to the top of the failure path as possible, and\ntest it by forcing a failure once.\n\nVerify: launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/<label>, then confirm last exit code = 0\nAND the real outcome exists and is fresh (the output file, message or row the job\nproduces) AND the run took a plausible time, not a few seconds; confirm you\nreceived the success notification. Then break the script on purpose and confirm\nyou receive the failure notification too. Silence should be impossible in both\ndirections, and so should a green exit code with nothing done.\n=== ./notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n---\n\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\n\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\ndatabase, no build step.\n\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\n\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\nconfusion.\n\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\n\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\nthe email are the confirmation, not the fetch.\n\nSimple things should stay simple.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a static site you can edit and deploy, and a Google account.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which spreadsheet to write into (or permission\nto create one), the address to notify, and the list of fields to collect.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create a Google Sheet for submissions. Its ID is the long string in the URL\n   between `/d/` and `/edit`.\n2. In that sheet choose Extensions, then Apps Script. Write a `doPost(e)` that\n   parses `e.postData.contents` as JSON; returns success immediately and writes\n   nothing if the honeypot field is non-empty; rejects missing required fields;\n   takes a `LockService.getScriptLock()` before writing so two submissions in\n   the same second cannot interleave; appends a row via\n   `SpreadsheetApp.openById(<id>).getSheets()[0].appendRow([...])` with a\n   timestamp first; releases the lock; then sends a notification with\n   `MailApp.sendEmail`. Truncate every field to a maximum length before writing.\n3. Deploy, New deployment, type \"Web app\", Execute as **Me**, Who has access\n   **Anyone**. Authorise it. Copy the URL ending in `/exec`.\n4. In the page, add the form with the real fields, plus a hidden input that no\n   human can see or tab into (`tabindex=\"-1\"`, `aria-hidden=\"true\"`) as the\n   honeypot, and a status element with `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"`.\n5. Validate in the browser first, setting `aria-invalid` on each field and\n   focusing the first bad one, so a screen reader announces the problem.\n6. Submit with `fetch(url, { method: \"POST\", mode: \"no-cors\", headers: {\n   \"Content-Type\": \"text/plain;charset=utf-8\" }, body: JSON.stringify(data) })`.\n   Do not use `application/json`: it triggers a preflight that Apps Script\n   never answers. Because the response is opaque, treat the resolved promise as\n   success and let the sheet and the email be the source of truth.\n7. Read `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign` from the query string, fall\n   back to `document.referrer`, and send that as a `source` field.\n8. When you later change the script, use Deploy, Manage deployments, the pencil\n   icon, and set Version to \"New version\". A fresh deployment would give you a\n   different `/exec` URL and silently break the live form.\n\nVerify: submit the form on the deployed site. A row appears in the sheet within\na few seconds and the notification email arrives. Then fill the honeypot field\nfrom devtools and submit again: no new row appears, and the endpoint still\nreports success, so a bot sees nothing unusual.\n=== ./Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n=== ./Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\n=== ./Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\" \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n<plist version=\"1.0\">\n<dict>\n  <key>Label</key>\n  <string>com.vk.nightly-review</string>\n  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n  <array>\n    <string>/bin/zsh</string>\n    <string>/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh</string>\n  </array>\n  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>Hour</key>\n    <integer>2</integer>\n    <key>Minute</key>\n    <integer>30</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err</string>\n  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>PATH</key>\n    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>\n  </dict>\n</dict>\n</plist>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0119DTXzw3uJ8shKttiXmWjG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
